49
00:03:59,713 --> 00:04:02,465
What do I need to remember?

50
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:10,054
Slim base image, requirements before code, --no-cache-dir, an unprivileged user.

51
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:15,769
PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 makes logs appear in docker logs immediately.

52
00:04:16,333 --> 00:04:23,223
Run a production server (gunicorn, uvicorn), not the framework's development server.

53
00:04:24,967 --> 00:04:28,406
How does containerizing a Go application work?

54
00:04:28,967 --> 00:04:31,260
Go compiles to a single binary.

55
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:33,035
Containerizing it means:

56
00:04:33,035 --> 00:04:38,582
compile inside the official Go image (no Go installation on your computer),

57
00:04:38,582 --> 00:04:43,168
with the module files copied first so dependencies are cached,

58
00:04:43,168 --> 00:04:48,420
as a static binary (CGO_ENABLED=0), and run it as an unprivileged user.

59
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:53,968
This lesson builds a working single-stage image and measures what it costs:

60
00:04:53,968 --> 00:04:57,666
the whole Go toolchain ships with an 8 MB program.

61
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:03,359
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

62
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:05,499
Let's run it.

63
00:05:06,033 --> 00:05:08,673
How big is the program itself?

64
00:05:09,233 --> 00:05:11,873
How big is the program itself?

65
00:05:13,633 --> 00:05:15,632
Let me try something.

66
00:05:15,632 --> 00:05:19,533
Dockerfile.broken forgets to copy go.mod:

67
00:05:20,067 --> 00:05:20,294
go:

68
00:05:20,294 --> 00:05:25,377
go.mod file not found in current directory or any parent directory:

69
00:05:25,377 --> 00:05:30,384
the error comes from the Go tool inside the RUN step (#8 0.236 go:

70
00:05:30,384 --> 00:05:31,902
…), not from Docker.

71
00:05:31,902 --> 00:05:36,681
Docker only reports that the step's command exited with code 1.

72
00:05:36,681 --> 00:05:44,571
A modern Go build needs the module file, and /src in the image only contains what the Dockerfile copied.

73
00:05:46,333 --> 00:05:55,033
Copy go.mod (and go.sum, when the module has dependencies) before building, as Dockerfile does:

74
00:05:56,767 --> 00:05:57,480
Got it.

75
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:00,231
What do I need to remember?

76
00:06:00,767 --> 00:06:09,666
Build inside golang:1.26-alpine; copy go.mod/go.sum first and run go mod download for caching.

77
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:16,064
CGO_ENABLED=0 produces a static binary that runs on any Linux image.

78
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:20,746
A single-stage Go image carries the whole toolchain:

79
00:06:20,746 --> 00:06:24,014
hundreds of MB for a program of a few MB.

80
00:06:25,767 --> 00:06:29,316
Why would I need containerizing a Java application?

81
00:06:29,867 --> 00:06:32,375
How to containerize a Java service:

82
00:06:32,375 --> 00:06:39,038
compile and package it into an executable jar inside an official JDK image (Eclipse Temurin),

83
00:06:39,038 --> 00:06:46,920
run it with java -jar as an unprivileged user, and understand how the JVM sizes its memory inside a container.

84
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:54,229
The example is a plain JDK HTTP server, so the build needs no Maven download; the Spring Boot pattern,

85
00:06:54,229 --> 00:06:59,316
which follows the same principles, is shown as an annotated Dockerfile.

