88
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:09,925
The JVM is container-aware:

89
00:07:09,925 --> 00:07:15,116
it reads the container's CPU and memory limits, not the host's.

90
00:07:15,116 --> 00:07:22,119
Start the image with a 512 MB memory limit and ask the JVM for its maximum heap size:

91
00:07:22,667 --> 00:07:25,213
The JVM is container-aware:

92
00:07:25,213 --> 00:07:31,156
it reads the container's CPU and memory limits, not the host's.

93
00:07:31,156 --> 00:07:39,173
Start the image with a 512 MB memory limit and ask the JVM for its maximum heap size:

94
00:07:39,173 --> 00:07:41,531
134217728 bytes = 128 MB:

95
00:07:41,531 --> 00:07:48,416
by default the JVM uses only 25 % of the container's memory for the heap.

96
00:07:50,167 --> 00:07:51,608
Let me try something.

97
00:07:51,608 --> 00:07:58,676
A colleague writes the start command by hand, as they remember it from the project's run configuration:

98
00:07:59,233 --> 00:08:02,124
Could not find or load main class main:

99
00:08:02,124 --> 00:08:06,868
the JVM started, opened app.jar, and found no class called main.

100
00:08:06,868 --> 00:08:11,316
Java class names are case-sensitive and include the package.

101
00:08:11,316 --> 00:08:17,098
List what the jar really contains, and which main class its manifest declares:

102
00:08:18,833 --> 00:08:27,038
Use the exact class name, or better, let the manifest decide with java -jar (what the image's CMD does):

103
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:29,513
Got it.

104
00:08:29,513 --> 00:08:32,264
What do I need to remember?

105
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:39,594
Compile and package inside an official JDK image; run with java -jar as an unprivileged user.

106
00:08:40,133 --> 00:08:47,263
The JVM respects container limits; by default the heap is 25 % of the container's memory.

107
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:55,010
Set -XX:MaxRAMPercentage (or JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS) instead of a fixed -Xmx.

108
00:08:56,767 --> 00:09:01,186
What should I know about containerizing a PHP application?

109
00:09:01,733 --> 00:09:05,797
A PHP application usually runs as two containers:

110
00:09:05,797 --> 00:09:12,265
PHP-FPM, which executes PHP code and speaks the FastCGI protocol on port 9000,

111
00:09:12,265 --> 00:09:21,056
and Nginx, which receives HTTP requests, serves static files itself and forwards .php requests to PHP-FPM.

112
00:09:21,056 --> 00:09:26,114
Each container does one job, the PHP image contains the code,

113
00:09:26,114 --> 00:09:30,758
and the two find each other by name on a shared network.

114
00:09:30,758 --> 00:09:35,983
This is also how Laravel and Symfony applications are deployed.

115
